## **Module 11 — AI Sound Engineer**

This is the feature that can differentiate our product from existing audio applications.

[EchoJay — AI-Powered Mix Feedback](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/xph5oW8XviDn6Ubt5gnaceApATt_lwu02hHojWenOhsOg2vBo1_sjzB-JZrH2UZi50W34xvfqfDbsSGySMsQ9xRxm8QXf7JZnY2rcE-_-0Zs_CDwvFtbJqsm9PWtO-_E3miAUc4kW-UAcwWKeW6zioKBn-HKIYnjvQzhrVUajA0?purpose=inline)

[echojay.ai](https://www.echojay.ai/support?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

I would build the AI around five intelligence capabilities.

**A. Diagnostic intelligence**

The AI interprets measurements and identifies likely causes.

Example:

> The bass channel has excessive low-frequency noise. The noise is concentrated around the mains frequency and its harmonics. Check the instrument cable, DI box and electrical grounding before applying EQ.

**B. Recommendation intelligence**

The application proposes changes and explains why.

Example:

> The preacher's voice sounds muddy. A measured buildup around 220 Hz may be contributing. Try a small reduction and compare the result.

**C. Configuration intelligence**

The AI can generate:

- EQ starting presets.
- Compressor configurations.
- Speaker delays.
- Crossover configurations.
- Routing suggestions.
- Livestream processing chains.

**D. Predictive intelligence**

It identifies recurring problems and warns operators before service.

Example:

> Feedback has repeatedly occurred at approximately the same frequency during the last three services. Investigate the microphone and speaker positions.

**E. Conversational intelligence**

A volunteer can ask:

- Why is the preacher's microphone unclear?
- Why is the bass guitar humming?
- Why does the back of the church sound quiet?
- How can I improve the choir?
- Why is the livestream distorted?

The assistant should respond using the actual measurements, device configuration and previous diagnostic history when available.

### **The intelligence architecture**

I would not make a general-purpose LLM responsible for processing live audio.

Instead:

| Intelligence layer     | Responsibility                                 |
| ---------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| DSP engine             | Deterministic audio processing                 |
| Signal analysis engine | Actual audio measurements                      |
| Rules engine           | Reliable engineering checks                    |
| Diagnostic models      | Pattern and anomaly detection                  |
| AI reasoning layer     | Interpret evidence and explain recommendations |
| Safety engine          | Validate and authorize changes                 |

This is important because a language model can make convincing but incorrect technical suggestions.

All changes should be validated against the actual DSP configuration, and dangerous changes should require operator approval.

## **Module 12 — System Diagnostics and Reliability**

The application must monitor its own health.

Features:

- Device disconnection detection.
- Audio-engine crash detection.
- Sample-rate mismatch warnings.
- Buffer-underrun monitoring.
- Audio clipping detection.
- USB interface failure alerts.
- CPU and memory monitoring.
- Configuration backup.
- Automatic recovery.
- Event logs.
- Service reports.
- Safe fallback profiles.

I would consider this module as important as the AI module.

A beautiful sound application that crashes during preaching is not a professional sound application.

# **4. The recommended technology stack**

This is where we need to make careful architectural decisions.

I recommend a **Windows-first native desktop application with a web-based interface and a dedicated real-time audio-processing engine**.

Do not build the entire audio engine in React or a browser.

## **4.1 Recommended stack**

| Layer          | Recommended technology                       | Purpose                               |
| -------------- | -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| Desktop shell  | Tauri 2                                      | Windows desktop application           |
| Frontend       | React + TypeScript                           | Application interface                 |
| UI styling     | Tailwind CSS                                 | Consistent design                     |
| Backend        | Rust                                         | Device management and system services |
| DSP engine     | CamillaDSP                                   | Audio processing                      |
| Audio analysis | Rust DSP libraries + REW integration         | Measurements                          |
| Audio capture  | WASAPI / ASIO                                | Hardware communication                |
| AI services    | Python + local/remote models                 | Diagnosis and recommendations         |
| Local database | SQLite                                       | Configuration and measurements        |
| Configuration  | JSON/YAML                                    | DSP profiles and device definitions   |
| Visualization  | Lightweight charting and waveform components | Live analysis                         |
| Livestream     | OBS WebSocket integration                    | Broadcast management                  |
| Cloud backend  | Optional Fastify API                         | Multi-church management               |
| Authentication | Better Auth                                  | User and organization accounts        |
| CI/CD          | GitHub Actions                               | Testing and distribution              |

### **Why Tauri?**

Tauri combines a web frontend with a native Rust backend. This makes it suitable for a small, efficient desktop control application. Its architecture also allows us to use native system capabilities while maintaining a modern interface.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://tauri.app\&sz=32)

Tauri+1



### **Why CamillaDSP?**

CamillaDSP already provides a configurable processing pipeline, filter support, channel mixing and an API that can be used for custom integrations. It also supports Windows and multiple audio backends.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.camilladsp.com\&sz=32)

CamillaDSP+1



### **Why not use JUCE as our first choice?**

JUCE is a strong professional audio development framework, especially for building plugins and custom audio hosts.

However, its current framework licensing is dual AGPLv3/commercial. A proprietary commercial product would need careful licensing decisions.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://github.com\&sz=32)

GitHub+1



I would therefore initially use Tauri + Rust + CamillaDSP. If we later develop proprietary low-latency audio plugins or a custom audio engine, we can evaluate JUCE or another appropriate framework with a proper licensing assessment.

### **Important Windows audio-engineering decision**

WASAPI is useful for compatibility, while ASIO is often preferable for professional audio interfaces requiring low latency.

We should support both where the hardware and driver permit it.

We must also make buffer sizes configurable and display measured latency. We should never promise zero latency.

CamillaDSP documents both Windows audio modes and the limitations of shared-mode audio processing.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.camilladsp.com\&sz=32)

camilladsp.com+1



# **5. System architecture: how all the components communicate**

The most important architectural decision is to separate the application interface from the real-time audio engine.

A user should be able to close a dashboard window without accidentally stopping the audio engine. The engine should be independently supervised.

