# **12. Quality assurance and professional acceptance criteria**

This is where I would be particularly strict.

A sound application must be tested as an audio product, not merely as ordinary business software.

| Test                | Acceptance requirement                                        |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Audio stability     | No unexplained dropouts during an extended stress test        |
| Clipping protection | Configured limiter prevents unintended digital overload       |
| Device recovery     | Recovers safely from device disconnection                     |
| Configuration       | Invalid DSP configurations are rejected                       |
| Presets             | Saved configurations can be restored accurately               |
| Latency             | Measured and displayed for supported processing routes        |
| Audio integrity     | No unexplained channel swapping or sample-rate errors         |
| AI recommendations  | Evidence and confidence are displayed                         |
| Feedback diagnosis  | Musical notes are not automatically classified as feedback    |
| Hum diagnosis       | Mains hum is distinguished from legitimate instrument content |
| OBS integration     | No feedback loops or duplicate capture                        |
| Security            | No unauthorized remote changes                                |
| Offline mode        | Core sound operation remains available without internet       |

### **The most important acceptance test**

We should compare the audio before and after processing.

For example:

- Preacher intelligibility.
- Noise floor.
- Frequency balance.
- Clipping incidence.
- Feedback stability.
- Livestream loudness consistency.
- Speaker coverage.
- Reverberation.

We should not claim an improvement simply because the application displays a green status indicator.

