It prevents multiple developers or AI agents from working on conflicting parts of the application.

For example:

- One agent works on the DSP engine.
- Another develops the dashboard.
- Another develops acoustic measurements.
- Another develops the AI diagnostic system.
- Another develops the OBS integration.

All communicate through documented contracts.

I would establish these rules:

1. No module directly modifies another module's internal implementation.
2. All hardware integrations use documented interfaces.
3. Every DSP change has automated tests.
4. All AI-generated configurations are validated.
5. No experimental DSP code is merged directly into production.
6. Every release must pass audio stability tests.
7. Dependency licences must be checked and recorded.
8. The repository must contain reproducible build instructions.

# **15. Biggest technical risks and how to manage them**

I want to be honest about what will be difficult.

| Risk                                 | Severity | Mitigation                                                  |
| ------------------------------------ | -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| Windows audio-driver incompatibility | High     | Test WASAPI and ASIO on real hardware                       |
| Audio dropouts                       | High     | Dedicated audio engine and watchdog                         |
| Audio latency                        | High     | Measure, configure buffers and avoid unnecessary processing |
| Incorrect AI recommendations         | High     | Evidence-based rules and human approval                     |
| False feedback detection             | High     | Require temporal and contextual evidence                    |
| Different mixer architectures        | High     | Modular device adapters                                     |
| Hardware control limitations         | High     | Explicit capability reporting                               |
| Incorrect automatic room correction  | High     | Calibrated measurement and verification                     |
| Third-party dependency licensing     | Medium   | Dependency and SBOM audit                                   |
| AI/cloud expenses                    | Medium   | Local-first design and optional cloud services              |

### **Three things I would specifically avoid**

**1. Building a browser-only audio engine.**

The browser can provide an interface, but professional live audio requires native device access, careful buffering and predictable processing.

**2. Letting AI directly modify live audio without safeguards.**

The AI should recommend changes. The DSP engine should validate them. The operator should approve important changes.

**3. Trying to replace the physical mixer immediately.**

Your MG16XU should remain the working mixer during the first stages. We should expand hardware compatibility gradually.

